Inferno copy of Berkeley SoftFloat 3
====================================

This is John R. Hauser's Berkeley SoftFloat 3 (BSD licence, see COPYING.txt;
the sources are those of the Berkeley mirror, which include additions such
as bfloat16 made after Release 3e), reduced to what Inferno needs.  The upstream README
is kept as README.txt and the upstream documentation in doc/.

SoftFloat is an IEEE 754 implementation written in portable C.  Inferno
uses it so that `real' arithmetic, constants and elementary functions give
the same bits on every machine, whatever its floating-point hardware.

What was kept
-------------

  float64_t  add, sub, mul, div, rem, sqrt, roundToInt, comparisons
             (quiet and signaling), conversions to and from 32 and 64 bit
             integers (signed and unsigned), and f32 <-> f64
  float32_t  the same operations: single precision is kept so that the
             single precision libraries of the Amiga (mathieeesingbas and
             mathieeesingtrans) do not have to go through double
  extFloat80_t  the same operations plus conversions to and from f32, f64
             and integers; not used by Dis, reserved for the 68881 FPU of
             the 68k emulation

123 source files remain (54 for f64, 30 more for f32, 39 more for extF80).
Removed: half precision and bfloat16 (f16, bf16), 128-bit floats, the fused
multiply-add (the real arithmetic of Dis never contracts operations), the
"M" (pointer argument) interface and everything it needs, the other NaN
specialisations, and the Makefiles and build directories.  Anything removed can be brought
back from the git history.

What was resolved once and for all
----------------------------------

The configuration macros of the upstream sources were resolved with
unifdef(1) and are gone, together with the code of the branches not taken:

  - SOFTFLOAT_FAST_INT64 is always on: 64-bit integers are required;
  - SOFTFLOAT_INTRINSIC_INT128, SOFTFLOAT_BUILTIN_CLZ and opts-GCC.h are
    gone: no compiler extension, no __int128, no builtin;
  - SOFTFLOAT_FAST_DIV32TO16 and FAST_DIV64TO32 are off (plain C division
    is not used);
  - SOFTFLOAT_ROUND_ODD is off: round-to-odd is not supported;
  - LITTLEENDIAN is gone: the order of the fields of the multiword types is
    fixed, most significant word first (`struct uint128 { v64, v0 }',
    `struct extFloat80M { signExp, signif }').  It is a matter of
    declaration only: the arithmetic never depends on the byte order of the
    host, and these structures must be accessed by field name, never as
    raw memory.  The results are the same with either order.

The only platform file left is source/include/platform.h, which defines how
functions defined in headers are declared (INLINE).  It replaces the
build/<target>/platform.h of the upstream distribution.

NaN policy
----------

The RISCV specialisation is used (source/RISCV): the default NaN is the
positive quiet NaN (0x7FF8000000000000 for float64_t), any operation with a
NaN operand returns it (the payload is not propagated) and the invalid
exception is raised for a signaling NaN.  Tininess is detected after
rounding.  This choice does not depend on any CPU: it is the one with the
simplest rules.

State
-----

softfloat_roundingMode, softfloat_exceptionFlags, softfloat_detectTininess
and extF80_roundingPrecision are global.  Inferno saves and restores them
for each process; SoftFloat itself raises flags and never traps (traps are
emulated by the caller, from the flags).

Testing
-------

test/regress.c runs deterministic pseudo-random vectors through every
operation and prints a hash of all results and flags.  The value expected
(e8f3b4a677c83197) is the one of the unmodified upstream sources and is
identical on 32 and 64 bit builds, at -O0, -O2 and -Os, and in strict C99.
Any change to this directory must keep it.
